WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.900
This video was made possible by Wix.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.910
Build your website easily at wix.com/go/wendover.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:12.740
This is a 737 MAX—a brand new airplane…
almost.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:16.449
It has a few aspects that makes it unique.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:19.800
Most modern airplane have electronic pre-flight
checklists.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.460
The 737 MAX uses paper checklists.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.980
Most modern airplanes can be turned on by
flipping a switch.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.570
The 737 MAX uses a seven step process.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.829
Most modern airplanes have electronic on-screen
warnings describing exactly what a problem

00:00:33.829 --> 00:00:34.829
is.

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:38.000
The 737 MAX only has a warning light.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.370
Most modern airplanes are fully fly-by-wire
meaning their ailerons and elevators and other

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:46.829
flight control surfaces move by electronic
signals triggering servo motors.

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:51.829
The 737 MAX has, running from the cockpit,
physical cables themselves pulling most of

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:54.149
the flight control surfaces to move.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:58.210
Not all of these design elements are necessarily
bad things—many pilots, in fact, prefer

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.450
the fly-by-cable system, for example—but
there is one other big difference between

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.640
most modern airplanes and the 737 MAX.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:11.640
Most modern airplanes are flying up above
right now, the 737 MAX is not.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.790
These design elements are all indicators of
a truth about the 737 MAX—it’s a 50 year

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:22.420
old airplane retooled, re-engined, and redesigned
three times to make it what it is today.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:27.830
It is the oldest major commercial plane-type
still being made today and it shows.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:33.180
Much of of the design of the 737 MAX was done
by people not even alive today.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.080
The reason Boeing revamped and revamped and
revamped this airplane over and over and over

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.970
again has to do with the economics of the
aviation industry as a whole and of one of

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:47.280
the world’s fiercest rivalries—Boeing
versus Airbus.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.830
Back in 2011, rumblings were emerging that
Boeing would design an all-new single-aisle

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:54.759
airplane to replace the rather old 737.

00:01:54.759 --> 00:01:58.570
It seemed like the logical choice that would
allow the manufacturer to design a far more

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:03.869
efficient aircraft unconstrained by the 737’s
old, metallic airframe and technologies.

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:08.110
The reason Boeing might not have chosen to
scrap the 737 earlier, though, is because,

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.319
to airlines, fuel efficiency matters less
for their short-haul planes.

00:02:12.319 --> 00:02:16.330
That’s partially just because smaller planes
fly fewer hours per day.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.870
Among American airlines, for example, the
average small narrow-body plane spends 8.2

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:26.000
hours airborne per day while the average wide-body
plane spends 11.5 hours airborne.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.350
Meanwhile, though, the average small, narrow-body
plane flies 4.5 flights per day whereas the

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.870
average wide-body flies just 1.5 per day.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:39.060
Considering small planes fly shorter routes,
they spend more time on the ground unloading

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.819
and loading between flights.

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:45.520
So, with fewer hours per day in the air, the
proportion of cost devoted to fuel is lower

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.670
to operate small planes than big planes.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:53.060
Traditionally, airlines would rather have
a cheap, small plane rather than an expensive,

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.850
super efficient small plane.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:58.750
The 737 is just that—it’s a cheap, small
plane.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:04.780
The 737-700’s list price is only $89 million
and, once airlines actually negotiate an order,

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.050
the real price is far less.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:11.850
With over 50 years of experience, Boeing has
perfected the 737 manufacturing process and

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:15.400
is therefore able to offer the plane at a
relatively cheap price.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.819
At their peak, Boeing was making one 737 every
14 hours.

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:24.710
So, in summary, when prioritizing which planes
to buy, airlines are going to be more focused

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:29.150
on upgrading their long-haul, wide-body fleets
to the newest, most fuel-efficient airplanes

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.450
than their small planes.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.420
In the past decade or so, though, airlines
have been changing how they’ve been using

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.349
narrow-body planes.

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:41.730
Planes like the 737, a320, and 757 have long
been the core of almost any airlines’ short-haul

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.570
fleet, but they’ve started to be used for
longer and longer flights.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:49.080
These smaller planes are cheaper to operate
and have been getting redesigned with longer

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.640
and longer range which allow airlines to operate
longer routes with less demand that couldn’t

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.520
fill a larger, wide-body plane.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.720
The fleet age of United’s planes, for example,
indicates just this.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.050
You can see that the average age of most of
their narrow-body fleet is from the late 1990’s

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:08.220
and early 2000’s while much of their wide-body
fleet was put into service in the current

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.220
decade.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.830
But going back to what’s changed, you can
see that with small, narrow-body planes, over

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:16.900
the past twenty years, these planes are flying
fewer and fewer flights per day while being

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:21.719
airborne for more and more hours per day meaning
they’re flying longer and longer routes.

00:04:21.719 --> 00:04:26.909
In addition, in 2011, when Boeing was deciding
what to do next with the 737 program, jet

00:04:26.909 --> 00:04:30.669
fuel prices had reached close to an all-time
high so airlines were more concerned than

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:34.050
ever about fuel efficiency, even for their
smaller planes.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:39.069
Therefore, the fuel efficiency of these small,
narrow-body planes was and is becoming more

00:04:39.069 --> 00:04:40.409
and more important.

00:04:40.409 --> 00:04:45.360
That, in addition to a general need to improve
efficiency over time, was why Boeing was looking

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.729
to create an all new narrow-body airplane.

00:04:48.729 --> 00:04:50.530
But then reality hit.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:55.270
On December 1, 2010, Airbus announced that
it would release a re-engined, more efficient

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:59.440
version of their A320 family aircraft—the
A320neo.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.500
Unfortunately for Boeing, the A320neo sold
quite well in its first few months.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.680
It was clear that airlines wanted a more efficient
small plane, and fast.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.849
Soon after, something worse happened.

00:05:10.849 --> 00:05:14.819
American airlines, which was, at the time,
one of the world’s largest airlines, had

00:05:14.819 --> 00:05:17.680
no Airbus planes in 2011.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.710
All of their long haul fleet and most of their
short-haul fleet was built by Boeing.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:29.360
On July 20, 2011, though, American announced
a massive order for 460 narrow-body planes—130

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:37.400
Airbus a320’s, 130 Airbus a320neo’s, 100
737’s, and 100 re-engined 737’s.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.069
The press release said, American, “intends
to order 100 of Boeing's expected new evolution

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:46.669
of the 737NG, with a new engine that would
offer even more significant fuel-efficiency

00:05:46.669 --> 00:05:48.559
gains over today's models.”

00:05:48.559 --> 00:05:53.379
At this point, this re-engined 737 had not
been announced.

00:05:53.379 --> 00:05:57.270
American announced an order for a non-existent
plane.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:03.089
They strong-armed Boeing into re-engining
their 737 rather than building a new airplane

00:06:03.089 --> 00:06:07.069
by dangling an order of this hypothetical
revamped airplane over them.

00:06:07.069 --> 00:06:12.090
It’s likely that Boeing had already agreed
internally to re-engine the plane but nonetheless,

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:14.289
why would American do that?

00:06:14.289 --> 00:06:20.150
Why would they want a less efficient, less
modern re-engined 737 over a more modern,

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.400
more efficient brand new narrow-body plane?

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.590
Well, in short, it’s because fuel cost is
far from all the cost.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:32.500
Pilots are generally trained to operate just
one type of aircraft.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:38.110
Some fly the a380, some fly the 777, some
fly the a320, and some fly the 737, but they

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:46.860
don’t fly the 737-700 or 737-800 or 737-900,
they fly the 737 airplane as a whole.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.860
Pilots are trained to the aircraft, not the
specific aircraft variant.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:56.099
In general, pilots can and do switch between
variants of aircraft even in any given day

00:06:56.099 --> 00:07:00.219
and are not required to do any substantial
training to be certified on different aircraft

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:01.219
variants.

00:07:01.219 --> 00:07:06.569
Crucially, the FAA has never required that
737 pilots complete any simulator training

00:07:06.569 --> 00:07:09.209
before flying new 737 variants.

00:07:09.209 --> 00:07:15.550
That’s because a 50 year-old 737 more or
less flies like a 1 year-old 737.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:20.580
American wanted a refreshed 737 over a new
plane since they already had thousands of

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:23.689
trained and certified 737 pilots.

00:07:23.689 --> 00:07:27.849
They had no pilots for a hypothetical new
narrow-body Boeing and the cost of training

00:07:27.849 --> 00:07:30.969
their pilots to a new plane would be massive.

00:07:30.969 --> 00:07:34.879
Even ignoring the training costs, though,
it’s far cheaper for airlines to just have

00:07:34.879 --> 00:07:39.669
one large fleet of one type of aircraft rather
than a few smaller fleets of different aircraft.

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:44.849
That’s why budget airlines like Ryanair,
EasyJet, and Southwest each operate only one

00:07:44.849 --> 00:07:45.940
aircraft type.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:50.580
It costs less to just have one group of pilots
and mechanics that can work on all the planes

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:55.219
and that’s why most airlines try to have
as few different aircraft types as possible.

00:07:55.219 --> 00:08:01.059
So, soon after American’s order announcement,
Boeing officially announced the 737 MAX.

00:08:01.059 --> 00:08:05.589
This aircraft would be 15% more fuel efficient
than the previous 737 model and would therefore

00:08:05.589 --> 00:08:12.719
have a maximum range of 4,400 miles or 7,100
kilometers compared to the old 737’s 3,400

00:08:12.719 --> 00:08:14.879
mile or 5,400 kilometer range.

00:08:14.879 --> 00:08:19.449
This would make it easily possible to operate
transatlantic flights on this small, narrow-body

00:08:19.449 --> 00:08:20.449
plane.

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:25.220
Airlines did end up taking advantage of this
range to operate long, yet low-demand routes.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:29.679
For example, Air Canada operated transatlantic
flights from Halifax and St. John’s to London

00:08:29.679 --> 00:08:32.180
Heathrow using the 737 MAX.

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:36.379
Norwegian Airlines flew them as far as Newburgh,
New York to Bergen, Norway.

00:08:36.379 --> 00:08:40.919
Gol Airlines, meanwhile, broke records with
their daily 737 MAX flight all the way from

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:45.029
Brasilia, Brazil to Miami, Florida—an up
to eight hour flight.

00:08:45.029 --> 00:08:49.329
To achieve the fuel efficiency and range improvement
that made these flights possible, though,

00:08:49.329 --> 00:08:52.130
Boeing had to make a crucial change to the
aircraft.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:55.190
They had to fit bigger engines.

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:58.990
Without getting too much into the engineering,
the newest, most efficient engines are all

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:03.740
physically larger than those of the past due
to designs that have more air bypass and flow

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:05.600
past the engine core.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:10.490
The problem, though, was that the 737 design
was quite low to the ground.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:15.220
Back 50 years ago, when the aircraft was designed,
this was a conscious choice.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:19.230
It was a feature that made it easy to load
bags without a conveyor belt and maintain

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:21.140
engines without a lift.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:24.769
This simplified operating to many of the less
developed airports of that time.

00:09:24.769 --> 00:09:29.330
This proximity to ground was no problem for
the first generation of 737 that had long

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.709
and thin engines that looked like this.

00:09:31.709 --> 00:09:36.399
For the second generation, though, the 737
Classic, Boeing moved the engine further forward

00:09:36.399 --> 00:09:40.740
and up and designed its intake as non-circular
to achieve more ground clearance.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:45.750
The next generation also kept this design,
but then for the 737 MAX, they just had to

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:47.920
fit an even larger engine.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:53.010
So, they put it even further forward and up
in addition to adding 8 inches or 20 centimeters

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:54.300
to the nose-gear.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.390
These changes, though, changed the aerodynamics
of the plane and therefore how it handled.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:03.120
It would cause the plane to pitch up more
than the last generation which would put it

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.209
at a greater risk of stalling.

00:10:05.209 --> 00:10:11.029
So, they installed a system called MCAS—the
Maneuvering Characteristics Augmentation System.

00:10:11.029 --> 00:10:14.610
This system would activate when it sensed
the aircraft’s angle of attack was too high

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:18.411
and would automatically rotate the horizontal
stabilizer to cause the aircraft’s nose

00:10:18.411 --> 00:10:19.720
to pitch down.

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:23.759
The aircraft figures out its angle of attack,
though, through a sensor and if this sensor

00:10:23.759 --> 00:10:30.069
is broken, it can cause the aircraft to think
it is flying at too high of an angle and self-correct.

00:10:30.069 --> 00:10:35.600
Unless the system is deactivated, it can keep
pitching the aircraft down until the ground.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.180
It is this that is believed to have been the
cause of the crash of Lion Air Flight 610

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:46.060
in October, 2018 and the crash of Ethiopian
Airlines Flight 302 in March, 2019 and it

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:51.330
is these that led to the grounding of every
single 737 MAX in the world.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:56.680
Boeing found itself, back in 2011, in a position
where it had to choose between the easy wrong

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:57.959
or the difficult right.

00:10:57.959 --> 00:11:03.250
It had to choose between short-term and long-term
gain and it chose to win now.

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:07.480
It chose to push the limits of an old design
to a point where it required correction upon

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:10.790
correction to even make it the same plane.

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:15.589
Given the storm of economic pressures surrounding
the company in 2011, though, Boeing’s investors

00:11:15.589 --> 00:11:19.350
would not have been happy if the company had
embarked on a long, expensive new aircraft

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:23.959
design process while Airbus engulfed market
share with their A320neo.

00:11:23.959 --> 00:11:27.889
Nobody would have guessed at the time, with
the company and aircraft’s phenomenal safety

00:11:27.889 --> 00:11:31.850
record, that that decision would lead to a
potentially faulty plane.

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:36.000
Nobody would have guessed that the decision
would potentially cost hundreds of lives.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:42.980
It was a hasty decision made by hasty human
brains and for that, Boeing and its 737 program

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:46.920
will suffer long-term, possibly permanent
consequences.

